    "updated_at": "2025-06-16T16:54:15Z",
    "body": "Building on top of @sorvell's observation, here're some additional behavior from safari tech preview 221\n```\nregistry.define('some-element', ScopedSomeElement);\nregistry.define('other-element', ScopedOtherElement);\nconst div = document.createElement('div',{customElementRegistry: registry});\n// made the following multi-line for the legibility\ndiv.innerHTML =\n  '<other-element id=\"foo\">\n    <other-element id=\"bar\">\n      <div id=\"baz\">\n        <other-element id=\"nested-ce\"></other-element>\n      </div>\n    </other-element>\n  </other-element>\n  <div id=\"cool\"></div>\n  <another-element></another-element>'\n```\n\nIt seems like the inheritance (not sure if this is the best term for this) of the scoped registry from the parent div only applies to the direct descendant with defined custom element in the given registry. So, in the above case, `#foo`, `#bar` got `div`'s registry, whereas the nested built-in element descendant `#baz`,  nested within another built-in element descendant `#nested-ce`, built-in element child `#cool`, not defined custom element child `another-element` all didn't get the registry from div",
